ace_id": "step_26", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_19", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_20", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_21", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_22", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_28", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_31", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_11", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_12", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_13", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_02", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_04", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_27", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_29", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "Members' activity is staggered in time (some silent early, others silent late), but their interaction partners are not: every active member in every sub-window contacts the same fixed ~30-node core, with only isolated one-off extras. Peak before/after Jaccard dissimilarity over candidate change points is ~0.14-0.27, well under the 0.6 threshold. The apparent shift is a change in who is active (volume), not a replacement of contacts, so no abrupt regime switch of partners is present."}, "raw_final_response": "**Analysis**\n\nThe group's *activity* is strongly staggered \u2014 individual members switch on and off (e.g. 658 is silent in 28052\u201338051, bursts in 38052\u201343051, near-silent after; 1220/1418/1676/1838 are silent early and active only after 48052; steps 11\u201313). That looks like a regime change if you only look at volume.\n\nBut the *partner sets* are the criterion, and they are essentially invariant. Every active member, in every sub-window across the whole period, talks to the same ~30-node core {150, 204, 207, 224, 230, 262, 487, 640, 788, 823, 913, 916, 992, 1010, 1132, 1165, 1186, 1294, 1308, 1347, 1372, 1520, 1556, 1575, 1590, 1698, 1728, 1798, 1805, 1938}: node 327 early (step_23), 411/464/556 mid-early (steps 16\u201318), 556/876/674 mid (steps 25, 26, 30), 1220/1418 late (steps 19, 20), 1676/1838 latest (steps 21, 22). Only sporadic singletons differ (681, 93, 305, 652, 1889, 1840, 242/700/1803, 1477, 186/1361/1705).\n\nTaking the group-union contact set on each side of any candidate change point with a 5000-unit half-window, the before/after sets share the full 30-node core; the union adds at most a handful of one-off partners, giving a peak dissimilarity of roughly 0.14\u20130.27 (steps 28, 31) \u2014 far below the 0.6 threshold. There is no interval where an old contact set is replaced by a disjoint new one.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_d9748f02\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [327, 390, 411, 464, 556, 658, 674, 876, 1220, 1418, 1676, 1838],\n  \"window\": [28052, 58052],\n  \"confidence\": 0.86,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_23\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_17\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_18\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_30\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_25\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_26\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_19\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_20\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_21\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_22\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_28\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_31\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_27\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\